# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[solemnly]] [[reject#Verb|reject]] (someone or something); to [[abandon#Verb|abandon]] (someone or something) [[forever#Adverb|forever]]; to [[disavow]], to [[disclaim]], to [[repudiate]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|renounce|Thesaurus:repudiate}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''abjure''' errors}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer Q1|act=I|scene=i|sig=A3|page=6|passage=[[w:Hermia|''Her''{{quote-gloss|''mia''}}]]. {{...}} But I beſeech your Grace, that I may knovve / The vvorſt that may befall mee in this caſe, / If I refuſe to vved ''[[w:Demetrius (A Midsummer Night's Dream)|Demetrius]]''. / [[w:Theseus|''The''{{quote-gloss|''seus''}}]]. Either to dy the death, or to '''abiure''', / For euer, the ſociety of men.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|lines=1115–1117|sig=Dd2|verso=1|page=213|passage=She diſappeerd, and left me dark, I vvak'd / To find her, or for ever to deplore / Her loſs, and other pleaſures all '''abjure''': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay Edinburgh Review|volume=I|page=32|passage=[B]oth [[w:Charles I of England|Charles {{quote-gloss|I}}]] himself and his creature [[w:William Laud|{{quote-gloss|William}} Laud]], while they '''abjured''' the innocent badges of Popery, retained all its worst vices, {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1855|author=[[w:Thomas Lake Harris|Thomas L[ake] Harris]]|chapter=Part Fourteen|title=An Epic of the Starry Heaven|edition=4th|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Partridge & Brittan,{{nb...|342 Broadway.}}|page=180|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/epicofstarryheav00harriala/page/180/mode/1up|oclc=6878086|passage=Only as thou '''abjurest''' self, / Lovest thy Brother more than pelf, / And drivest out the impish elf, // Sectarian pride, from all thy heart, / Canst thou have place, or lot, or part / Within the Heaven-created mart {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1902|author=[[w:Robert Marshall (dramatist)|Robert Marshall]]|chapter=About Myself|title=The Haunted Major|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Grant & Murray{{nb...|Ltd., 126 Princes Street, Edinburgh.}}|year_published=1932|page=17|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/hauntedmajor0000robe/page/17/mode/1up|oclc=752709593|passage=[S]he is an ardent golfer. Except during the season in town, she spends her year in golfing, either at St. Magnus or Pau, for, like all good Americans, she has long since '''abjured''' her native soil.}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} To [[renounce#Verb|renounce]] (something) upon [[oath#Noun|oath]]; to [[forswear]]; specifically, to [[recant]] or [[retract#Verb|retract]] (a [[heresy]] or some other [[opinion#Noun|opinion]]); to [[withdraw#Verb|withdraw]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|disavow|renounce|Thesaurus:recant}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''abjure''' allegiance to a prince}}
##* {{RQ:Thomas More Workes|title=Heresyes|book=I|chapter=I|page=108|column=2|passage=[T]he man ye write of, was of many thinges borne wrõge {{quote-gloss|wrong}} in hande, & therin ſo ſore handeled that he was forced to for ſwere and '''abiure''' certaine hereſyes, & openly to penance therfore, where he neuer held any ſuch.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=V|scene=i|page=16|column=2|passage=Graues at my command, / Haue vvak'd their ſleepers, op'd, and let 'em forth / By my ſo potent Art. But this rough Magicke / I heere '''abiure''': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Burke American Taxation|page=36|passage=[T]he conceſſion vvas far ſhort of ſatisfying the principle vvhich Lord Hillſborough had '''abjured'''; or even the pretence on vvhich the repeal of the other taxes vvas announced: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Beckford Vathek|page=39|passage=VVouldeſt thou devote thyſelf to me? Adore then the terreſtrial influences; and '''abjure''' [[w:Muhammad|Mahomet]].}}
##* {{RQ:O'Keeffe Zenobia|volume=II|page=247|passage=[W]hen, guided by learned rabbis, invincible generals, and sublime and enlightened heathens, thou in evil hour '''abjuredst''' the gospel, in outward act if not in heart, and ungratefully dismissed thy faithful adviser, thou didst seal the fate of thy country and thyself.}}
##* {{RQ:Southey All for Love|page=120|passage=I wrote to him, saying, 'all these things, Lord, he hath '''abjured''' before me; admit him, therefore, if it pleaseth thee.' But he hath written back, 'unless he '''abjureth''' all this in writing, and in his own hand, I will not admit him.'}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (someone) to recant or retract (a heresy or some other opinion). {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Fuller Worthies of England|part=1|chapter=Bark-shire|page=90|passage=[T]hey vvere betrayed, and then many of them to the number of ſix or ſeven ſcore vvere '''abjured''', and three or four of them burnt. Novv although vve knevv not hovv to call theſe Martyrs vvho ſo ſuffered, ''their Names'' no doubt ''are vvritten in the Book of Life''.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|law|historical}}
### ''Especially in'' '''[[abjure the realm]]''': to [[swear#Verb|swear]] an oath to [[leave#Verb|leave]] (a [[place#Noun|place]]) forever.
###* {{RQ:Hobbes English Works|volume=VI|page=141|passage=When a clerk heretofore was convicted of felony, he might have saved his life by '''abjuring''' the realm; that is, by departing the realm within a certain time appointed, and taking an oath never to return. But at this day all statutes for abjuration are repealed.}}
###* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|book=IV|chapter=Of Offences against God and Religion|page=56|passage=[A]s a feme-covert recuſant may be impriſoned, ſo all others muſt, vvithin three months after conviction, either ſubmit and renounce their errors, or, if required ſo to do by four juſtices, muſt '''abjure''' and renounce the realm: and if they do not depart, or if they return vvithout the king's licence, they ſhall be guilty of felony, and ſuffer death as felons.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} To cause or [[compel]] (someone) to leave a place forever; to [[banish]].
###* {{RQ:Drayton Barrons Wars|stanza=15|page=6|passage=The ſtrong effect of theyr conceiued ire, / Vrging the vveake King vvith a violent hand, / T' '''abiure''' thoſe falſe Lords from the troubled land.}}
###* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|book=III|sectionname=Cent[ury] XIV. To [[w:Clement Throckmorton (MP for Warwickshire)|Clement Throckmorton]], the Elder, of Haseley in Warwick-shire, Esq|page=90|passage=About this time a ſubject brought in a ''Bull of Excommunication'' againſt another ſubject of this Realm, and publiſhed it to the Lord Treaſurer of ''England'', and this vvas by the ancient Common-Law of ''England'' adjudged Treason againſt the King, his Crovvn and dignity, for the vvhich, the offender ſhould have been dravvn and hanged, but at the great inſtance of the chancelour and Treaſurer, he vvas onely '''abjured''' the Realm for ever.}}
###* {{RQ:Strype Church of England|volume=I|chapter=The Lord Keeper's and Mr. Speaker's Speeches. A Second Parliament. Matters Transacted Relating to Religion.{{nb...|The Penalty of High Treason in the Bill for the Supremacy, Argued. Speeches of the Lord Mountague, and Mr. Atkinson a Lawyer, against It. Another for It. Acts Passed: Viz. for the Assurance of the Queen's Royal Power: Against Conjurations: For Excution of the Writ for Taking a Person Excommunicated, &c. The Queen's Answer about Her Marriage.}}|page=269|passage=In the Time of King [[w:Edward III of England|''Edward'' III.]] one ſhould have been hanged, dravvn and quartered, for publiſhing an Excommunication, directed from the Biſhop of ''Rome'' againſt one of the King's Subjects. But at the Entreaty of the Lord Chancellor and Lord Treaſurer, his Life vvas pardoned; notvvithſtanding he vvas '''abjured''' the Realm.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## To solemnly reject; to abandon forever.
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|lines=470–473|page=26|passage=Eaſily canſt thou find one miſerable, / And not inforc'd oft-times to part from truth; / If it may ſtand him more in ſtead to lye, / Say and unſay, feign, flatter, or '''abjure'''?}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} To recant or retract a heresy on oath.
##* {{RQ:Thomas More Workes|title=Heresyes|book=IV|chapter=XVI|page=282|column=1|passage=Nor neuer yet found I ani. j. {{quote-gloss|any one}} but he would once '''abiure''', though he neuer intended to kepe his [[oath|othe]].}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author=w:Umberto Eco|chapter=|translator=w:William Weaver|title=w:The Name of the Rose|series=A [[w:Helen Wolff|Helen]] and [[w:Kurt Wolff (publisher)|Kurt Wolff]] Book|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harcourt (publisher)|Harcourt Brace Jovanovich]]|page=238|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nameofrose000ecou/page/238/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-15-144647-6|passage=And going toward the Justice Gate, many said to him, "Recant! Recant! Don't insist on dying," and he said, "Christ died for us." {{...}} At the Field of Justice, one said to him he should do as a certain monk, his superior, had done, '''abjuring''': but Michael answered that he would not '''abjure''', and I saw many in the crowd agree and urge Michael to be strong: so I and many others realized those were his followers, and we moved away from them.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|law|historical}} To swear an oath to leave a place forever.
##* {{RQ:Palsgrave Lesclarcissement|book=3rd|chapter=The Table of Verbes|folio=cxxxvi|verso=1|passage=I '''Abiowre'''&#11805; I forſake myne errours as an heretyke dothe&#11805; or forſwere the kynges landes&#11805; ''ie abiure, prime coniugationis''.}}
##* {{RQ:Foxe Actes and Monuments|edition=6th|volume=II|chapter=The Historie of Diuers Good Men and Women, Persecuted for Religion in the Citie and Dioces of the Bishop of London, Briefly Extracted out of the Registers of [[w:Richard FitzJames|Richard Fitziames]]|page=736|column=2|passage=Well to bee ſhort, what for the cauſes before recited, as alſo for that they had once alreadie '''abiured''', and yet (as they terme it) fell againe into relapſe, they were both (as you haue heard) in the end burned together in Smithfield: {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1789|author=[[w:John Kelynge|John Kelyng&#91;e&#93;]]|chapter=High-Treason|editor=[[w:John Holt (Lord Chief Justice)|[John] Holt]]|title=A Report of Divers Cases in Pleas of the Crown, Adjudged and Determined in the Reign of the Late King [[w:Charles II of England|Charles II]].{{nb...|With Directions for Justices of the Peace and Others. Collected by Sir John Kelyng, Knt. Late Lord Chief Justice of His Majesty’s Court of King’s Bench. From the Original Manuscript under His Own Hand. To which is Added, the Report of Three Modern Cases, Armstrong and Lisle—The King and Plummer—The Queen and Mawgridge. As Originally Published by Lord Chief Justice Holt. With Additional Notes and References, by G[eorge] J[oseph] Browne, Barrister at Law.}}|location=Dublin|publisher={{...|Printed by}} P. Byrne,{{nb...|No. 108, Grafton-street.}}|page=36|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DDxFAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA36|oclc=|passage=One '''abjureth''' and is after taken in ''England'', and demanded what he could ſay, why Execution ſhould not be awarded, he ſtands mute, he ſhall be hanged, and not put to penance or preſſed; becauſe he was attainted of the Felony before by his Confeſſion: For he cannot have the Benefit of the Sanctuary to '''abjure''', unleſs he confeſs the Felony which is entered on Record by the Coroner; {{...}}}}

